WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.670
Speaker 1: Well, the pro-gun right has gone
absolutely nuts after the assassination of

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.190
the former Japanese prime minister Shinzo
Abe.

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:12.839
Now, the assassination itself is a major,
major political story.

00:00:12.839 --> 00:00:17.670
It's been widely covered over the last three
days, and there's not so much to add about

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:18.670
that.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.590
It's a story of global significance and Japanese
significance, etc..

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.390
And most of what there is to say about that
has been said.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:31.920
I want to focus on the reaction to this assassination,
which was done with a firearm from the pro-gun

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.711
right in the United States who has rallied
around this assassination to say, look, gun

00:00:37.711 --> 00:00:43.610
control district in Japan and a former prime
minister was assassinated.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.770
And this proves that it doesn't matter what
you do in terms of guns.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.600
This proves that restricting guns doesn't
make a difference.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.130
And of course, they're completely wrong.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.930
And we're going to look at the data and we're
going to make an empirical case for why that

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:54.930
is.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.860
Now, just to sort of set the stage, what we
have learned about this assassination, this

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.970
is per NPR, political motivations have been
denied by the shooter.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.960
He told police the attack had been planned
for months.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.100
Abe was shot from behind in the western city
of Nara.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.240
While speaking at a campaign event.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.750
Police arrested Tatsuya Yamagami, a 41 year
old at the scene.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:23.940
Yamagami, an unemployed former sailor in the
maritime armed forces of Japan, confessed

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.840
to the killing for which he used a handmade
firearm, according to police.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:34.240
And that's an element of the reaction that
we are hearing from many, which is, look,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.549
even if you restrict firearms completely to
the point of making them illegal, people will

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:41.250
just make them at home.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.800
And we're going to get back to that.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.190
But the most important top line thing to understand
is that although there has been a killing,

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.950
this is you know, last week we had this caller
who said, David, why why aren't anecdotes

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.439
okay to understand the world around us?

00:01:57.439 --> 00:01:58.750
Anecdotes are our experience.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:05.600
And the point I made was if you extrapolate
from an anecdote, you often will be wrong.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.899
And the fact that someone was killed with
a gun in Japan misses the reality that the

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:15.130
rate of gun death in Japan is a fraction of
what it is in the United States.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.390
The numbers are actually quite stunning.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.900
Two important bits of data.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.970
First of all, very few people own guns in
Japan.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.220
And I think no, nobody denies that part.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.220
In the U.S.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:33.489
There's an estimate of 120 guns per 100 people
that that's more than one gun per person in

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:38.209
the United States, whereas in Japan, at 0.3
guns per 100 people.

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:39.920
So that's the gun ownership rate.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.870
And then if you continue in looking at this
BBC article, you see that gun deaths in Japan

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:44.870
are extremely rare.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:51.750
In the U.S., there are seven deaths by firearm
per 100,000 people, whereas in Japan it's

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:59.430
0.03. Japan's rate of gun death is a fraction.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:04.980
And in fact, the U.S. rate of gun death is
23,000% higher than the rate in Japan.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.650
That's not a typo, 23,000% higher.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:14.680
So then you go to, well, the person made the
gun at home, so you can't prevent that.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:20.080
Well, imagine if the only way to shoot someone
was to make the gun at home.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.410
Think about that, if you could.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:25.100
If that was really the way if you wanted to
shoot someone, you had to make a firearm in

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.659
your house.

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:29.790
Imagine how much lower gun violence would
be because most people aren't going to do

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:30.790
that.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:32.540
Guns are not illegal in Japan.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.330
They're just much more difficult to obtain.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.989
And this has worked at making the gun violence,
the gun death rate almost zero.

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:46.470
If to buy a gun in Japan, you need to take
a firearms class, pass a written exam, which

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.330
is only held three times a year.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.370
So you can't just say, well, I want it tomorrow.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.590
It's held three times a year.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:57.230
You've got to get a doctor's note saying you're
mentally fit and have no history of drug abuse.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:02.490
You have to ask for a permit to do firing
training that can take a month.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.060
You have to be interviewed by police and explain
why you need a gun.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.270
You have to pass a review of your criminal
history and gun possession, record employment,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:15.909
involvement with organized crime, debt, relationships
with friends, all these things you then apply

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:17.820
for a gun powder permit.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.780
You take a one day training class, have to
pass a firing test.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.270
At that point, you can get a certificate from
a gun dealer describing the gun that you want.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:32.490
If you are trying to hunt, you have to get
a hunting license to show I'm really getting

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.490
this.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.860
In order to hunt you then have to get a gun
safe and an ammo locker.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:40.460
The police have to inspect it and make sure
that it's in compliance.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.939
You then do a final kind of overall background
review.

00:04:43.939 --> 00:04:48.460
And after that step 13 or 18, depending on
whether you consider each of these things

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:52.330
one or sometimes two things, then you can
actually go and buy a gun.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.960
So the anecdotal reaction, this is the this
is the epitome of why anecdotes aren't useful.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.430
The anecdotal reaction is.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.550
Strict gun laws.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.909
And there's a shooting.

00:05:02.909 --> 00:05:04.979
None of the gun laws work.

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:07.589
The systemic empirical reaction is.

00:05:07.589 --> 00:05:09.529
Strict gun laws.

00:05:09.529 --> 00:05:11.289
Very small rate of gun ownership.

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:12.789
Guns aren't illegal.

00:05:12.789 --> 00:05:19.270
But all of this leads to a tiny, tiny rate
of gun death in the country of Japan.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:24.469
Yes, a person was killed with a gun and it
was notable and it was a known person because

00:05:24.469 --> 00:05:25.509
it's a former prime minister.

00:05:25.509 --> 00:05:30.560
It's like if Bill Clinton or George W Bush
were or Donald Trump were killed with a firearm,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:31.560
it's it is notable.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.960
It's major news.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:39.460
But the gun violence rate is nothing in Japan
compared to that of the United States.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.020
And that really should be the big takeaway.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:46.969
So I was involved in a whole bunch of different
Twitter spats as this was going on with people

00:05:46.969 --> 00:05:50.080
saying this this proves that you can't prevent
gun violence.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.180
No, actually, this proves that you can.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.960
And in fact, this story is a major story in
Japan, not only because of the person that

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.849
was killed, the former prime minister, but
because anyone was killed with a gun at all,

00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:04.360
because it is so rare in the entire country.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.539
And Japan has a large population, I think
it's about 80, 80 million.

00:06:08.539 --> 00:06:09.539
You have to adjust for that.

00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:14.819
It's smaller than the U.S., but that rate
is tiny for a country with so many people.

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:18.789
And in fact, let me just double check Japan,
population 125 million, actually.

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:24.589
So it's an even, even bigger country making
the number of gun deaths even smaller.

00:06:24.589 --> 00:06:25.589
That's the takeaway.

00:06:25.589 --> 00:06:25.939
Their policy has worked.

